sentences.l:
And down from a peg he hung the clear-sounding lyre
right above his head, and pointed it out for him to take in his hands,
the herald;

For so to him, prophesying, spoke Phoebus Apollo
in holy Pytho, when he crossed the stone threshold
to consult the oracle;

but Odysseus,
a great purple cloak taking in his sturdy hands,
drew it down from his head, and covered his fair face;

Truly, whenever the divine minstrel ceased singing,
a tear having wiped away, from his head the cloak he would take,
and, a double cup taking, would pour libations to the gods;
